    "turn_text": "passed i think you start uh... with the blocking and tackling of of the foundations right so in colorado where purple state we still were able to get universal background checks passed and nobody talks about repealing anymore uh... we passed limited magazine capacities we passed uh... red flags this last year I think you take those are the basic building blocks. You go to a couple of other purple states, a couple of red states. When you get with universal background checks, when you get the local statistics, so in Colorado, population 5 million people, in 2012 we only got to half the gun purchases. But there were 38 people who tried to buy a gun, who applied for a background check, and we stopped them. That implies that there were another 38 people that bought a gun and didn't apply for a background check and they were able to buy a gun. When you give those statistics to Republican business people, they go nuts. They don't want anybody to shoot their kids or shoot themselves. Gun reform in Colorado came and fits and starts a little bit after Aurora though. It was a battle. We waited a few months and let the community process the loss, but then we went right out in 2013, and again, the Rocky Mountain Gunners Association, the NRA, came after us. The NRA went out through the Rocky Mountain Gunners Association. They said, if any Republican supports universal background checks. We will not only deny that Republican funding for any future campaign, but we will make sure there's a well-funded primary opponent in their next election. Well, no wonder I couldn't get a single Republican vote. Every single Republican business person that people I served on boards with, the civic leaders, all these Republicans, every one of them supported universal background checks. It was just the basic, almost like extortion that of the Rocky Mountain Gun Owners and the NRA, their trade associations. Their job is to do whatever they can to help their members do better. Well,